The planning fallacy was first proposed by [ Daniel Kahneman
](/wiki/Daniel_Kahneman "Daniel Kahneman") and [ Amos Tversky
](/wiki/Amos_Tversky "Amos Tversky") in 1979.  [6]  [7]  In 2003, Lovallo and
Kahneman proposed an expanded definition as the tendency to underestimate the
time, costs, and risks of future actions and at the same time overestimate the
benefits of the same actions. According to this definition, the planning
fallacy results in not only time overruns, but also [ cost overruns
](/wiki/Cost_overruns "Cost overruns") and [ benefit shortfalls
](/wiki/Benefit_shortfall "Benefit shortfall") .  [8]

