Sleep itself is a different story. Scientists have known for a while now that
shorter sleep is tied to dangerous diseases, [ like heart disease and stroke
](https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2017/08/170829091040.htm) . There is
mounting evidence that sleep deprivation leads to a [ higher risk of obesity
](https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/fullarticle/410832)
and [ Alzheimer’s disease
](http://www.nature.com/neuro/journal/v18/n7/abs/nn.4035.html?foxtrotcallback=true)
. Large population studies reflect a saddening truth—the [ shorter your sleep,
the shorter your life ](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4067693/)
. Not only that, sleep helps us to [ hold onto our memories
](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/behavioral-and-brain-
sciences/article/a-refined-model-of-sleep-and-the-time-course-of-memory-
formation/D65F856C772D4CAF6E352471B05B7953) and to [ learn
](http://learnmem.cshlp.org/content/11/6/705.short) facts and skills faster,
making it important for everyone including infants, students, athletes,
pilots, and doctors.

Learn [ how meditation can improve sleep
](http://greatergood.berkeley.edu/article/item/how_mindfulness_improves_sleep)
.

####  Gustatory system (taste)  [  [ edit
](/w/index.php?title=Sense&action=edit&section=29 "Edit section: Gustatory
system \(taste\)") ]

Within the structure of the [ lingual papillae ](/wiki/Lingual_papillae
"Lingual papillae") are [ taste buds ](/wiki/Taste_bud "Taste bud") that
contain specialized gustatory receptor cells for the [ transduction
](/wiki/Transduction_\(physiology\) "Transduction \(physiology\)") of taste
stimuli. These receptor cells are sensitive to the chemicals contained within
foods that are ingested, and they release [ neurotransmitters
](/wiki/Neurotransmitter "Neurotransmitter") based on the amount of the
chemical in the food. Neurotransmitters from the gustatory cells can activate
[ sensory neurons ](/wiki/Sensory_neuron "Sensory neuron") in the [ facial
](/wiki/Facial_nerve "Facial nerve") , [ glossopharyngeal
](/wiki/Glossopharyngeal_nerve "Glossopharyngeal nerve") , and [ vagus
](/wiki/Vagus_nerve "Vagus nerve") [ cranial nerves ](/wiki/Cranial_nerves
"Cranial nerves") .  [16]

